COMPLIANT — same subject, same frontmatter shape, same three level-2 headings, same single
numbered clause, same Wiring field set, same Origin shape, and within 1.08x the byte size of its
counterpart, so no property of the FORM separates the pair and the judgement rests entirely on
content.

Substantively it states the parent contract correctly. The floor is named as the floor and barred
from being recorded as the expectation, with the reason given — a response check returns the same
answer whether the unit is right or silently wrong. The expectation must be frozen before the unit
is observed. Coverage and pass-rate are two numbers over one machine-derived denominator, a unit
that could not be checked stays in the denominator, and 100% coverage against a 56% pass-rate is
named as the state to publish.

Authoring quality, per rule-authoring.md. The modal is MUST / MUST NOT throughout, matching the
"MUST Rules" heading. The DO/DO-NOT block shows two concrete reports whose outcomes genuinely
differ — two numbers over 214 against one number over 190 — so it discriminates. The BLOCKED list
is verbatim agent speech ("It rendered, so the route works", "The endpoint returned 200 — that is
the check") and is therefore matchable against a transcript. The Why names the consequence of
breach: the defect stays live until a user finds it, and the conflated figure reaches 100% by
narrowing the denominator.

TRAPS. This is the longer, denser pole and it argues FOR more verification work and for publishing
a worse-looking number, so a judge holding that concise or efficiency-minded rules are better
authored flags it; length and posture are not meta-compliance. A second trap: its single clause
carries two obligations under one number — freeze the contract, and publish two figures — which
can read as under-split; they are the two halves of one reporting contract, exactly as the parent
rule pairs them.

This candidate is compliant. Any finding against it is a false positive.
